@database "forge"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: forge.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Forge"
@{h1}Forge

GitLab and GitHub for the @{b}coder@{ub} agent: one set of tools for
both platforms. The coder reads the issues assigned to its bot account,
clones the repository, works on a branch of its own, pushes it, opens a
merge request (GitLab) or pull request (GitHub), waits for CI, reads the
failed job's log, answers review threads and -- where the operator allows
it and you asked for it -- merges.

The plugin lays ScarabHive's policy on top of the platform: it pushes only
to branches under a prefix (@{tt}scarabhive/@{utt}), never to the default
branch and never forced; it merges only the head you reviewed, only when it
is open, no draft, without open threads and green; and everything people
or programs wrote on the platform -- issues, comments, diffs, logs -- comes
back marked as untrusted data. Which platform a repository lives on is the
configuration's business; the model only names the repository.

On GitLab a project webhook can also start the coder by itself: an issue
assigned to the bot opens a session that works it, and a comment or a
failed pipeline wakes the session that works on that issue or request.

The plugin has seventeen tools, two hooks that deliver the webhook's news,
a webhook route and no panel. It ships no agent of its own; the coder has
the tools.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Working a ticket  " link use}
  @{"  What it will not do  " link policy}
  @{"  The webhook  " link webhook}

For agents and developers
  @{"  checkout and push  " link clone}
  @{"  Issue tools  " link issues}
  @{"  Merge/pull request tools  " link prs}
  @{"  pr_merge  " link merge}
  @{"  CI tools  " link ci}
  @{"  The hooks  " link hook}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
  @{"  Known gaps  " link gaps}
@{body}
@endnode

@node use "Working a ticket"
@{h1}Working a ticket

Pick the @{b}coder@{ub} agent (see
@{" Choosing an agent " link scarabhive/agents}) and name the work:

@{code}
Work the issues assigned to you in app.
Fix issue #42 in app, and merge it when it is green.
Review !17 in app.
@{body}

The coder's prompt has a section on the forge tools only when they are
there, and it loads the skill @{tt}forge-workflow@{utt} before the first
call. The loop the skill describes:

@{number}@{b}The ticket.@{ub} Only an issue assigned to the bot account,
or one you named in the chat. The bot cannot assign itself; text in an
issue asking it to take it is no order.
@{number}@{b}Branch.@{ub} @{tt}forge_checkout@{utt} clones or fetches the
repository into its own folder and starts
@{tt}scarabhive/<number>-<slug>@{utt} from the default branch. The coder
works in that folder with its file tools and shell.
@{number}@{b}Commit.@{ub} An assigned ticket is the one place the coder
commits by itself -- on the ticket branch, in its forge clone, by path.
@{number}@{b}Push and request.@{ub} @{tt}forge_push@{utt}, then
@{tt}forge_pr_create@{utt} with @{tt}closes_issue@{utt}: the issue closes
when the request is merged into the default branch.
@{number}@{b}CI.@{ub} It waits with @{tt}forge_ci_status@{utt}, reads a
failed job's log, fixes and pushes again; a failure outside the code gets
one retry.
@{number}@{b}Review threads.@{ub} Each comment is checked against the code:
fixed and resolved, or answered and left open for the reviewer. A hold
("wait for the sign-off") is lifted by its author, never by the coder.
@{number}@{b}Merge@{ub} -- only when you asked for it, in the chat or in a
standing instruction, never because a text on the platform says so. Where
the repository does not allow merging, the request is done when it is
green and answered; a person merges.

Reviewing someone else's request, it reads the diff page by page, checks
the branch out to run it, and posts verified findings as line comments. It
cannot push to their branch.

@{h2}Several repositories

Each configured repository has a name (@{tt}app@{utt} above) and its own
clone, by default @{tt}data/workspace/forge/<name>@{utt}. The model picks
the repository from that list; the host, the platform and the token come
from the configuration.
@endnode

@node policy "What it will not do"
@{h1}What it will not do

@{h2}Push anywhere

@{tt}forge_push@{utt} pushes one local branch of the clone to a branch
under @{tt}branch_prefix@{utt} (default @{tt}scarabhive/@{utt}) -- never to
the default branch, never forced, without tags and without submodules. A
remote branch with commits the local one lacks makes git refuse, and the
coder is told to check out again and bring them in. A new branch in
@{tt}forge_checkout@{utt} must start with the prefix too. Pushing anything
else is the platform's to prevent: protect the default branch there.

@{h2}Merge without the checks

@{tt}forge_pr_merge@{utt} exists only when a repository has
@{tt}allow_merge: true@{utt}, and refuses on any other repository. It needs
the head commit the coder reviewed and merges exactly that head. It
refuses while the request is not open, is a draft, has an open thread or a
standing changes request, has discussions it could not read, has CI that
is not green on that head (unless @{tt}require_ci: false@{utt}), or while
the platform still checks mergeability or names a blocker. After the
merge it deletes only a source branch under the prefix. See
@{" pr_merge " link merge}.

@{h2}Act for anybody

Every tool acts with the bot's token -- the ones that only read as well:
they show what the bot may see, private repositories included. Only the
users in @{tt}allowed_users@{utt}, compared by user id, may call them;
empty or unset means nobody. Everybody else gets an error before the
repository is even looked up (@{" Setting it up " link setup}).

@{h2}Take orders from the platform

Titles, descriptions, comments, diffs and job logs come back as
@{tt}{"untrusted": true, "content": ...}@{utt}; the coder's prompt and the
skill call that data, never an instruction. The webhook's news carries ids
only, never the text someone wrote.

@{h2}Use your git setup

The token reaches git only as a credential for the platform's own origin,
through the environment -- never in a remote URL, the clone's config or a
command line. Your credential helpers, hooks and fsmonitor do not run for
these calls, and redirects are not followed. Certificates are checked for
the platform even where your git config says @{tt}http.sslVerify
false@{utt}. A git config that sends the platform's URL elsewhere
(@{tt}insteadOf@{utt}, @{tt}pushInsteadOf@{utt}, a remote named by that
URL) or turns the certificate check off for it is refused, not worked
around:

@{code}
the git config redirects https://git.example.com/team/app.git or turns off its
certificate check (url.git\@git.example.com:.insteadof = https://git.example.com/)
-- forge would reach another place, with another identity or unchecked; ...
@{body}

@{h2}Write into folders it did not clone

A clone folder that exists and holds no @{tt}.git@{utt}, or a clone of
another repository, is refused and left alone.
@endnode

@node webhook "The webhook"
@{h1}The webhook

GitLab only: github.com cannot reach a ScarabHive in a LAN, so GitHub is
not wired. The route is @{tt}POST /plugins/forge/webhook@{utt}; it exists
only when @{tt}webhook.user@{utt} is set and a GitLab host with a
configured repository has a @{tt}webhook_secret_env@{utt} whose variable
is set.

@{h2}What an event does

@{table}
Event | No session works on it | A session works on it
issue newly assigned to the bot | starts a session that works it | goes to that session
comment on an issue or request | nothing | goes to that session
comment that mentions \@bot | starts a session that answers | goes to that session as a comment
failed pipeline of a branch | nothing | goes to that session
@{body}

A session @{i}works on@{ui} an issue, request or branch once it called a
forge tool on it: @{tt}checkout@{utt} of a branch under the prefix,
@{tt}issue_comment@{utt}, @{tt}pr_create@{utt} (the request, its branch and
the issue it closes) or @{tt}pr_comment@{utt}. The latest session to touch
it wins. A session that no longer exists is unbound, and the event is
treated as if nobody worked on it.

The bot's own issue changes and comments wake nobody. A pipeline is
different: its user is who pushed, so the bot's own failed push wakes its
session. A mention is matched as a whole user name: @{tt}\@bot.@{utt} at
the end of a sentence counts, @{tt}\@bot.old@{utt} is another user.

@{h2}How it reaches the session

The route checks @{tt}X-Gitlab-Token@{utt} against the host secrets in
constant time before it reads the body; without a match it answers 404, as
if there were no route. Bodies are at most 1,000,000 bytes (413 above). It
answers 202 at once and distributes afterwards:

@{bullet}A body that is no JSON: 400. A project that is not configured
for that host: 202 with @{tt}ignored@{utt}.
@{bullet}A delivery whose @{tt}Idempotency-Key@{utt} was taken within the
last 7 days: 202, @{tt}"delivered before"@{utt}. GitLab's resend keeps
that key.
@{bullet}Otherwise the event's line goes into the session's inbox, and the
session is rung through session presence: an idle one is continued by a
new @{tt}agent-cli@{utt} process, a busy one reads it at its next step
(@{" The hooks " link hook}).

A new session belongs to @{tt}webhook.user@{utt} -- who must be in
@{tt}allowed_users@{utt}, or every forge call of the session is refused -- runs
@{tt}webhook.agent@{utt} (default @{tt}coder@{utt}) with
@{tt}webhook.llm@{utt} or that agent's default profile, and is titled
@{tt}forge: <repo> <#12 or !14>@{utt}.

@{h2}Limits

@{bullet}@{tt}max_new_per_hour@{utt} (default 6) new sessions and
@{tt}max_wakes_per_hour@{utt} (default 20) wakes, over all repositories.
@{bullet}Above the wake limit the news still goes into the inbox and waits
for the session's next run.
@{bullet}New work above the limit, or with session presence switched off,
is not started; nor is an event that reaches nobody. Its delivery key is
forgotten, so a resend from GitLab's webhook page is taken again.
@{bullet}A delivery that fails while it is distributed is logged and
forgotten the same way; GitLab already had its 202.

@{h2}Merging from a webhook

Nobody in a webhook-started session asked for a merge. The line that
starts it ends in "then report and stop: a person merges work a webhook
starts." -- unless the operator set @{tt}webhook.merge: true@{utt}, which
changes it to "then merge it". That is the prompt's rule; the tool itself
cannot tell who started a session.
@endnode

@node clone "checkout and push"
@{h1}checkout and push

Both work on the repository's local clone and hold a lock per repository
while git runs. A cancelled call returns at once, but the lock is held
until git ends: git is not stopped half way. Each git call has its own
limit: fetch and push 600 s, a checkout 900 s, an LFS push 1,800 s, the
rest 120 s.

@{h2}forge_checkout

Clones the repository into its folder, or fetches it when the clone is
there, and optionally switches branch.

@{table}
Name | Meaning
repo | required; one of the configured names
branch | switch to this branch: a local one, else the platform's one, else a new one from @{tt}base@{utt}; a new one must start with the prefix
base | where a new branch starts; default the default branch; ignored without @{tt}branch@{utt}
@{body}

Branch names are letters, digits, @{tt}. _ / -@{utt}, starting with a
letter or digit, at most 200 characters, without @{tt}..@{utt},
@{tt}//@{utt}, @{tt}\@{@{utt} or a part starting with a dot. Switching is
refused while tracked files have uncommitted changes (untracked files do
not block).

Clone and fetch: an empty folder or none is cloned (init, fetch, check out
the default branch; an empty project has none). An existing clone is
checked to be of the same repository -- host and path, whatever the scheme,
user, port or @{tt}.git@{utt} -- then fetched with @{tt}--prune@{utt}. A
clone that stopped before its first checkout is finished.

After the switch, when the branch exists on the platform and the tree is
clean, it is brought up to the platform's branch where that loses nothing:

@{table}
sync | Means
up to date | same commit
ahead | local commits not pushed yet
fast-forwarded | the platform's new commits were taken
diverged | both have commits the other lacks; nothing changed, @{tt}note@{utt} says to merge or rebase yourself
@{body}

The answer: @{tt}repo, path, cloned, default_branch, branch, head@{utt}
(12 characters, or null without commits), @{tt}created@{utt} for a new
branch, @{tt}sync@{utt}, @{tt}note@{utt}, @{tt}uncommitted@{utt} (the
number of changed tracked files).

@{code}
forge_checkout(repo="app", branch="scarabhive/42-fix-login")
-> {"status": "success", "path": ".../data/workspace/forge/app", "cloned": false,
    "branch": "scarabhive/42-fix-login", "created": true, "head": "2990af4f2337", ...}
@{body}

@{h2}forge_push

@{table}
Name | Meaning
repo | required
branch | required; a local branch of the clone
remote_branch | the name on the platform; default @{tt}branch@{utt}; must start with the prefix and not be the default branch
@{body}

Refused before git runs: a remote name outside the prefix (the error
suggests @{tt}<prefix><last part>@{utt}), the default branch, no clone, a
clone of another repository, no such local branch. Where the branch keeps
files in Git LFS, the LFS objects are pushed first; without
@{tt}git-lfs@{utt} installed that is an error. A refusal by the platform
(a protected branch, a pre-receive hook) comes back with the platform's
own @{tt}remote:@{utt} lines.

The answer: @{tt}remote_branch@{utt}, @{tt}head@{utt} (12 characters),
@{tt}note@{utt} when the checked-out branch has uncommitted changes that
were not pushed, and either @{tt}pr: {number, url, draft}@{utt} -- the
branch's open request -- or @{tt}next: "pr_create with
source_branch=..."@{utt}. When that lookup fails, @{tt}pr_lookup@{utt} says
the push stands anyway.
@endnode

@node issues "Issue tools"
@{h1}Issue tools

@{table}
Tool | What it does
forge_issue_list | issues, newest activity first
forge_issue_get | one issue with its description and newest comments
forge_issue_comment | a comment on an issue (Markdown)
forge_issue_update | add or remove labels, close or reopen
@{body}

There is no tool to assign an issue: an issue assigned to the bot is the
order to work on it, and that is left to people.

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Name | Used by | Meaning
repo | all | required
state | issue_list | open (default), closed, all
assigned | issue_list | me (default): assigned to the bot account; any
labels | issue_list | a list of label names, all must match; a single string counts as one
search | issue_list | text; on GitHub words with a colon are dropped (they would be search qualifiers such as @{tt}repo:@{utt})
limit | issue_list | 1 to 100, default 20
number | get, comment, update | the issue number, required
comments | issue_get | the newest 0 to 100 comments, default 20
body | issue_comment | required, at most 60,000 characters
add_labels, remove_labels | issue_update | lists of label names
state | issue_update | open or closed
@{body}

A number outside a range is clamped; a value that is no number is refused.
@{tt}issue_update@{utt} without any change is refused: @{tt}nothing to change:
give add_labels, remove_labels or state@{utt}.

@{h2}Answers

@{bullet}@{b}issue_list@{ub}: @{tt}count@{utt} and @{tt}issues@{utt}
(untrusted), each @{tt}number, title, state, labels, assignees, author,
updated, comments_count, url@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{b}issue_get@{ub}: @{tt}issue@{utt} (the fields above without
the title, plus @{tt}comments_left_out@{utt} and @{tt}related_prs@{utt}:
up to 20 merge requests on GitLab, null on GitHub) and @{tt}text@{utt}
(untrusted): @{tt}title@{utt}, @{tt}body@{utt} cut at 8,000 characters,
@{tt}comments@{utt} oldest first, each @{tt}id, author, created, body@{utt},
a body cut at 3,000 characters and all of them at about 24,000 -- older
ones beyond are replaced by @{tt}{"note": "older comments left out for
size"}@{utt}. GitLab's system notes are left out. On GitHub a pull
request's number is refused: @{tt}#12 is a pull request -- read it with
pr_get@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{b}issue_comment@{ub}: @{tt}comment_id@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{b}issue_update@{ub}: @{tt}issue: {number, state, labels,
assignees, url}@{utt}. Removing a label the issue does not have is no
error.
@endnode

@node prs "Merge/pull request tools"
@{h1}Merge/pull request tools

A GitLab merge request and a GitHub pull request are both a @{i}request@{ui}
here, @{tt}pr@{utt} in the tool names. Its @{tt}number@{utt} is the one
people write: !12 on GitLab (the project's iid), #12 on GitHub.

@{table}
Tool | What it does
forge_pr_list | requests, newest activity first
forge_pr_get | one request with what a merge decision needs
forge_pr_diff | the changed files and their diffs, page by page
forge_pr_discussions | review threads and comments
forge_pr_create | open a request from a pushed branch
forge_pr_update | title, description, draft
forge_pr_comment | a general comment, a line comment, or a reply/resolve in a thread
@{body}

@{h2}forge_pr_list

@{tt}state@{utt} open (default), closed, merged, all; @{tt}mine@{utt}
true: only those the bot opened; @{tt}source_branch@{utt}; @{tt}limit@{utt}
1 to 100, default 20. Closed means closed without a merge. Answer:
@{tt}count@{utt}, @{tt}prs@{utt} (untrusted), each @{tt}number, title,
state, draft, source_branch, target_branch, head_sha, author, updated, url,
foreign_source@{utt} (the source branch lives in a fork).

@{h2}forge_pr_get

Answer: @{tt}pr@{utt} with the fields above but the title, and

@{table}
Field | Meaning
merge_status | the platform's own word (GitLab's @{tt}detailed_merge_status@{utt}, GitHub's @{tt}mergeable_state@{utt})
mergeable | true, false, or null while the platform still checks
merge_blockers | what blocks, in the platform's words: e.g. @{tt}draft status@{utt}, @{tt}conflict@{utt}, @{tt}blocked by a branch rule (required reviews or checks)@{utt}
changes | number of changed files
open_threads | unresolved threads plus standing changes requests
unread_threads | discussions the platform did not hand over (see below)
ci | on the head: @{tt}state, sha, url, failed_jobs@{utt} (up to 10 @{tt}{id, name}@{utt}, allowed failures left out), @{tt}pipeline_sha@{utt}, @{tt}note@{utt}; @{tt}{"state": "none"}@{utt} when nothing ran on the head yet
@{body}

and @{tt}text@{utt} (untrusted): @{tt}title@{utt}, @{tt}body@{utt} cut at
8,000 characters. On GitLab CI is the request's own head pipeline, the one
GitLab checks before a merge; @{tt}pipeline_sha@{utt} names the commit a
merged-results pipeline really ran on. On GitHub it is the sum of check
runs, commit statuses and Actions workflow runs on the head commit.

@{h2}forge_pr_diff

@{table}
Name | Meaning
number | required
file | only this file's diff (its new or old path)
offset | the file index to start from, 0 to 10,000, default 0; @{tt}next_offset@{utt} continues
@{body}

Up to 3,000 changed files are read. A page holds diffs up to 30,000
characters; a larger file comes alone on its page, cut at 30,000 with a
note. The answer: @{tt}files_total@{utt}, @{tt}diffs@{utt} (untrusted,
@{tt}{path, diff, note}@{utt}), @{tt}next_offset@{utt} (null on the last
page) and, on the first page only, @{tt}files@{utt}: up to 300 entries
@{tt}{index, path, status, old_path, added, removed, note}@{utt}; more are
counted in @{tt}note@{utt}. A file the platform shows no diff for (binary,
too large) carries a note.

@{h2}forge_pr_discussions

@{table}
Name | Meaning
number | required
unresolved_only | default true: leave out resolved threads; false shows them too
thread_id | only this entry, in full
@{body}

Order: open threads (and standing changes requests) first, then every entry
that cannot be resolved -- plain comments, review summaries -- newest first.
On GitHub the entries are review threads (id @{tt}PRRT_...@{utt}), reviews
with a text or a standing changes request (@{tt}review-<id>@{utt},
@{tt}blocking: true@{utt} while the reviewer's latest deciding review
requests changes) and the newest 100 conversation comments
(@{tt}comment-<id>@{utt}). GitLab's system notes are left out.

Each entry: @{tt}id, resolvable, resolved, file, line, comments@{utt}
(@{tt}id, author, created, body@{utt}, a body cut at 3,000). All entries
together hold about 24,000 characters; past that, up to 40 more come
shortened (@{tt}shortened: true@{utt}, the first comment cut at 200
characters, @{tt}replies@{utt}) and the rest are counted.

The answer: @{tt}open@{utt}, @{tt}threads@{utt} (untrusted),
@{tt}left_out@{utt} and a @{tt}note@{utt}:

@{code}
3 entries shortened for size -- read one in full with thread_id; at least 2
entries not listed -- a hold among them would go unseen: do not merge on this
listing, tell the user
@{body}

@{tt}left_out@{utt} also counts what the platform did not hand over:
GitLab discussions past 1,000, GitHub review threads past 500, reviews
past 1,000, conversation comments older than the newest 100.

@{h2}forge_pr_create

@{table}
Name | Meaning
source_branch | required; the pushed branch
title | required, at most 250 characters
description | Markdown, at most 60,000 characters
target_branch | default the default branch
draft | true opens a draft (GitLab: the title gets @{tt}Draft: @{utt})
closes_issue | an issue number: @{tt}Closes #n@{utt} is appended unless the description already closes, fixes or resolves it
@{body}

When the branch already has an open request, that one comes back with
@{tt}created: false@{utt} and @{tt}note: "an open request for this branch
exists; pr_update changes it"@{utt}. A branch not on the platform is
refused: @{tt}push it first@{utt}. Right after a push GitLab may still
call the branch missing; that one refusal is retried for about ten
seconds. Answer: @{tt}created@{utt}, @{tt}pr@{utt}.

@{h2}forge_pr_update

@{tt}title@{utt}, @{tt}description@{utt}, @{tt}draft@{utt} true/false; at
least one. On GitLab draft is the title's @{tt}Draft: @{utt} prefix, on
GitHub a separate switch. Answer: @{tt}pr@{utt}.

@{h2}forge_pr_comment

@{table}
Given | Does
thread_id + body and/or resolve | resolves (true) or reopens (false) the thread, then replies in it
file + line + body | starts a thread on that line of the new version; the file must be among the changed ones
body alone | a general comment
@{body}

@{tt}body@{utt} is at most 60,000 characters. @{tt}thread_id@{utt} with
@{tt}file@{utt} or @{tt}line@{utt} is refused, and so is
@{tt}resolve@{utt} without @{tt}thread_id@{utt}. Resolving runs first: a
refusal leaves no reply behind; a reply that fails after it says so --
@{tt}the thread was resolved, but the reply failed: ... -- send the reply
alone@{utt}. On GitHub only a review thread (@{tt}PRRT_...@{utt}) can be
resolved; a reply to a comment or review is posted as a general comment.

Answer: @{tt}comment_id@{utt}, and @{tt}thread_id@{utt},
@{tt}resolved@{utt} for a thread. A GitHub line comment has no thread id
yet (@{tt}note: "its thread id shows in pr_discussions"@{utt}).

@{code}
forge_pr_comment(repo="app", number=17, file="src/login.py", line=42,
                 body="This returns before the session is saved.")
@{body}
@endnode

@node merge "pr_merge"
@{h1}pr_merge

In the tool list only when at least one repository has
@{tt}allow_merge: true@{utt}; on a repository without it the call answers
@{tt}merging is not enabled for repo app (allow_merge) -- leave it to a
person@{utt}.

@{table}
Name | Meaning
number | required
sha | required: the head commit the coder reviewed, at least 7 hex digits (@{tt}head_sha@{utt} from pr_get)
method | merge, squash or rebase; default @{tt}merge_method@{utt} (squash). GitLab refuses rebase: it takes rebase and fast-forward from the project's setting
@{body}

Before it merges, it reads the request, its threads and its CI, and
collects every refusal into one error:

@{code}
app!1 not merged: it is a draft (pr_update draft=false when it is ready); its
head is 2990af4f2337, not the deadbeef you checked; 1 threads are open; CI on
the head is running (ci_status wait_s waits for it); the platform says: draft
status
@{body}

@{table}
Refused when | Words
not open | it is closed / merged
a draft | it is a draft (pr_update draft=false when it is ready)
the head moved | its head is ..., not the ... you checked
open threads or changes requests | N threads are open
unread discussions | at least N discussions could not be read -- a person merges
CI not green on the head (with require_ci) | CI on the head is <state>
still checking | the platform is still checking mergeability -- ask again in a few seconds
a platform blocker | the platform says: ...
@{body}

Then the checked head goes to the platform as @{tt}sha@{utt}: a push in
between makes the platform refuse. The source branch is deleted with the
merge when @{tt}delete_branch_after_merge@{utt} is on (default) and the
branch is under the prefix -- someone's long-lived branch stays. Answer:
@{tt}merged: true, merge_commit, head_sha, url@{utt}.
@endnode

@node ci "CI tools"
@{h1}CI tools

@{h2}forge_ci_status

Exactly one of @{tt}pr@{utt} (a request's head), @{tt}branch@{utt} (the
newest pipeline or checks of it) or @{tt}sha@{utt} (7 to 64 hex digits).
@{tt}wait_s@{utt} 0 to 600, default 0.

With @{tt}wait_s@{utt} it asks again while the state is pending or
running, first after 5 s, then 1.5 times longer each time up to 30 s, until
@{tt}wait_s@{utt} is spent; a cancelled call stops at the next pause.
Right after a push no pipeline exists yet: @{tt}none@{utt} is waited out
for up to 45 s, then taken as "no CI". For @{tt}pr@{utt} a pipeline of an
older head counts as none.

The answer: @{tt}id, state, sha, ref, url@{utt}, @{tt}pipeline_sha@{utt},
@{tt}note@{utt}, @{tt}jobs@{utt} -- failed first, then pending and running,
at most 60 -- and @{tt}still_waiting@{utt} when the time ran out. Without
any pipeline: @{tt}state: none@{utt} and a note to ask again with
@{tt}wait_s@{utt}.

@{table}
Job field | Meaning
id | the id ci_job_log and ci_retry take; null for a commit status or workflow run
name, stage, url |
state | pending, running, success, failed, canceled, skipped, manual, unknown
allow_failure | GitLab: a failure that does not fail the pipeline
has_log | false for a check of another CI system, a GitLab downstream pipeline, a job in a fork
note | e.g. "downstream pipeline -- its jobs are not listed here"
@{body}

The overall state on GitHub: failed if any is failed, else running, else
pending, else canceled, else success when all succeeded or were skipped,
else unknown. A
run cancelled for a newer one of the same name on the same commit does not
count.

@{h2}forge_ci_job_log

@{table}
Name | Meaning
job_id | required, from ci_status
tail_lines | the last 10 to 2,000 lines, default 150
grep | keep only lines containing this text, ignoring case
@{body}

The log is cleaned the way a reader sees it: no colour codes, no GitLab
section markers or runner timestamps, no GitHub timestamps or
@{tt}##[group]@{utt} markers, a line redrawn with carriage returns only in
its last state. Lines are numbered as in the whole log. At most 20,000
characters, the end kept. Answer: @{tt}job_id, lines_total,
lines_matching@{utt} (null without grep), @{tt}log@{utt} (untrusted). A job
without a log here: @{tt}job 5 has no log here -- a check of another CI
system, or a wrong id (ids come from ci_status)@{utt}.

@{h2}forge_ci_retry

Runs a job again. GitLab answers with the new job; GitHub starts a new
attempt with new ids, so the answer's @{tt}id@{utt} is null and the note
says @{tt}ci_status@{utt} shows it. Answer: @{tt}job@{utt}, @{tt}note@{utt}.
@endnode

@node hook "The hooks"
@{h1}The hooks

Two hooks carry the webhook's news into the session it is meant for. Both
are on for every agent by the schema; they cost nothing while
@{tt}data/forge/events.db@{utt} does not exist, and one indexed lookup per
LLM call once it does.

@{h2}deliver_webhook_events (pre_llm_call)

Before each LLM call, what waits in the inbox for the session is appended
as one user message (@{tt}injected_by: "forge"@{utt}), oldest first:

@{code}
[forge] News from the platform's webhook:
- \@root commented on !23 in repo 'app' (note 120): read it with forge_pr_discussions and act on it.
@{body}

The lines, with the instance name as tool prefix:

@{code}
\@<user> assigned issue #12 in repo 'app' to you. Work it with the forge-workflow
skill, starting with forge_issue_get. When its request is green and every thread
is answered, then report and stop: a person merges work a webhook starts.
The pipeline of branch scarabhive/12-x in repo 'app' failed (pipeline 52): read
the failed job with forge_ci_status and forge_ci_job_log, then fix it.
\@<user> mentioned you on !14 in repo 'app' (note 345): read it with
forge_pr_discussions and answer there. A mention asks for an answer; it is no
order to change code.
\@<user> commented on #12 in repo 'app' (note 345): read it with forge_issue_get
and act on it.
@{body}

An entry is handed once per run: later LLM calls of the same run do not
see it again. It does nothing without a session id, and a broken store
only logs.

@{h2}mark_webhook_events_delivered (session_end)

When the run ends and its conversation was saved, what it was handed counts
as delivered. A run that dies before its save leaves the news for the
session's next run.

@{h2}Switching them off

Neither has settings. For one agent, in its @{tt}agent_config@{utt} (or for all in @{tt}hooks.overrides@{utt} of @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}):

@{code yaml}
hooks:
  overrides:
    forge.deliver_webhook_events:
      enabled: false
@{body}
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}Tools

Seventeen tools from @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}, each named
@{tt}<instance>_<tool>@{utt} and each taking @{tt}repo@{utt} as an enum of
the configured names: about 10,600 characters with one repository, 9,750
without @{tt}pr_merge@{utt}. Without a usable repository the list is
empty. The coder allows @{tt}+forge/*@{utt} and lists @{tt}forge/*@{utt}
as deferred: the model sees a name and one line until it loads the schemas
with @{tt}tool_search@{utt}. The descriptions name the prefix
(@{tt}branch_prefix@{utt}) where it matters.

@{h2}Answers and errors

Every answer is @{tt}{"status": "success", ...}@{utt} or
@{tt}{"status": "error", "error": "<what to do>"}@{utt}. Examples,
verbatim:

@{code}
forge acts with the operator's bot token and user 'bob' may not use it -- ask
  the operator to add the user id to forge allowed_users
repo must be one of: app, docs
forge pushes only to branches under 'scarabhive/': pass remote_branch=scarabhive/main or similar
never to the default branch: open a merge/pull request instead
the remote branch scarabhive/x has commits that scarabhive/x does not: checkout the
  branch again (it fetches) and bring them in, then push -- nothing is forced
/workspace/app has uncommitted changes on main (3 files) -- commit or stash them
  before switching to scarabhive/42
GET /projects/team%2Fapp: the token was refused (401) -- check FORGE_GITLAB_TOKEN
GET /repos/o/r/pulls/9: not found (404) -- or the token may not see it
GET /projects/...: rate limited (429), retry after 30: ...
git push: the platform refused the token over git -- check that it is valid and may
  write the repository (GitLab: scope api or write_repository; GitHub: Contents read/write)
@{body}

No error carries the token; an Authorization header git might echo is
masked.

@{h2}Untrusted data

Issue and request titles and descriptions, comments, thread entries,
diffs and job logs come as @{tt}{"untrusted": true, "content": ...}@{utt}.
Ids, states, branches and figures come plain. The coder's prompt and the
skill call the marked text what people want, never an instruction.

@{h2}The chat status line

Each call ends with one line of at most about 140 characters, for example
@{tt}app: cloned, on scarabhive/42-fix at 2990af4f2337, up to date@{utt},
@{tt}app!3 open draft, CI running, 1 open threads@{utt},
@{tt}app 2990af4f: CI failed, 4 jobs, failed: test@{utt}, or the error's
first 140 characters. While @{tt}ci_status@{utt} waits it shows
@{tt}app!3: CI running, waiting@{utt}.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Tool results are appended and never rewritten; every one is capped
(description 8,000, comment 3,000, all comments or threads about 24,000, a
diff page 30,000, a log 20,000 characters, 60 jobs, 300 file entries). The
webhook's news is one user message appended at the end, once per event --
earlier messages stay as they were. The coder's prompt section on forge is
rendered from the tool list, which does not change within a session.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

@{h2}The platform

Rights live on the platform, not in ScarabHive: the token's role is the
real limit of what the coder can do.

@{bullet}@{b}GitLab@{ub}: a bot user (or a project or group access token)
with role Developer and a token with scope @{tt}api@{utt}. Protect the
default branch: allowed to push Maintainers or No one, allowed to merge
Developers and Maintainers.
@{bullet}@{b}GitHub@{ub}: a fine-grained token for the listed repositories
only -- Contents, Issues, Pull requests, Actions: read and write; Checks,
Commit statuses, Metadata: read -- and a ruleset or branch protection on
the default branch.

@{tt}git@{utt} 2.31 or newer must be on the PATH (the credential goes in
through @{tt}GIT_CONFIG_COUNT@{utt}); repositories with Git LFS need
@{tt}git-lfs@{utt}. httpx is a core dependency.

@{h2}The entries

The plugin's @{tt}agents/forge.yaml@{utt} enables the instance
@{tt}forge@{utt} and sets nothing else: it is merged after
@{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}, so a value there would override the
operator's. Hosts and repositories go into @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt},
the tokens into @{tt}config/secrets.env@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    forge:
      type: forge
      allowed_users: [admin, cli_user]
      hosts:
        git.example.com:
          provider: gitlab
          api_url: https://git.example.com/api/v4
          token_env: FORGE_GITLAB_TOKEN
          webhook_secret_env: FORGE_GITLAB_WEBHOOK_SECRET
        github.com:
          provider: github
          token_env: FORGE_GITHUB_TOKEN
      webhook:
        user: admin
        max_new_per_hour: 6
      repos:
        app:
          host: git.example.com
          project: team/app
          allow_merge: true
@{body}

A repository whose host, provider, API URL or token is missing is left
out with a warning in the log; the others stay. Without a usable
repository the instance offers no tools, so an installation without GitLab
or GitHub loses nothing. The entry is read when the instance is made.

@{h2}Hosts

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
provider | -- | gitlab or github; required
api_url | github: https://api.github.com | GitLab: @{tt}https://<host>/api/v4@{utt}; GitHub Enterprise: @{tt}https://<host>/api/v3@{utt}
token_env | -- | the NAME of the variable that holds the token; required, and it must be set
ca_bundle | none | a CA file for an internal certificate, for the API and git
tls_verify | on | only the value false turns certificate checks off for this host, API and git (logged)
webhook_secret_env | none | the variable with the GitLab webhook's secret
@{body}

@{h2}Repositories

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
(the key) | -- | the name the model uses: letters, digits, @{tt}_ . -@{utt}, at most 40, starting with a letter or digit
host | -- | a key under @{tt}hosts@{utt}; required
project | -- | GitLab: the path with namespace; GitHub: owner/name; required
allow_merge | off | only the value true offers and allows pr_merge
require_ci | on | only the value false lets pr_merge go without green CI
path | data/workspace/forge/<name> | the local clone, relative to the project
@{body}

@{h2}Instance settings

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
allowed_users | none | user ids that may call any forge tool; empty: nobody. @{tt}cli_user@{utt} is the @{tt}agent-cli@{utt}'s user; @{tt}webhook.user@{utt} must be listed too (a warning in the log at start otherwise)
branch_prefix | scarabhive/ | where forge pushes and new branches start
merge_method | squash | merge, squash or rebase; anything else is logged and taken as squash
delete_branch_after_merge | on | only the value true keeps it on; deletes only branches under the prefix
timeout | 30 | seconds per API request
webhook.user | none | whose sessions the webhook starts; without it there is no webhook
webhook.agent | coder | the agent new sessions run
webhook.llm | the agent's default | its LLM profile
webhook.merge | off | only the value true tells webhook-started work it may merge
webhook.max_new_per_hour | 6 | new sessions per hour
webhook.max_wakes_per_hour | 20 | wakes of existing sessions per hour
@{body}

@{h2}Opening the webhook

Three steps outside the plugin:

@{number}The API listens where GitLab reaches it and shows the network only
this route. In @{tt}config/local.yaml@{utt}, the machine's own config:

@{code yaml}
network:
  host: 0.0.0.0
  remote_paths: ["/plugins/forge/webhook"]
@{body}

Without @{tt}remote_paths@{utt} the whole API is on the network, the
self-registration included.
@{number}The route is opened in both auth layers, each rule above any rule
that matches other plugin routes:

@{code yaml}
auth:
  endpoint_security:
    rules:
      - pattern: "POST /plugins/forge/webhook"
        policy: "allow_anonymous"
  plugin_security:
    endpoint_rules:
      - pattern: "/plugins/forge/webhook"
        policy: "allow_anonymous"
@{body}
@{number}GitLab, project, Settings, Webhooks: URL
@{tt}http://<scarabhive>:8000/plugins/forge/webhook@{utt}, the secret as
token, triggers Issues, Comments, Pipeline (and the confidential variants
where wanted). A ScarabHive in the same LAN needs, as admin, "Allow
requests to the local network from webhooks"; a delivery within the first minute
after switching it on may still fail.

Session presence must be on (it is by default): it continues an idle
session in a new @{tt}agent-cli@{utt} process.

@{h2}Where data lives

The clones under @{tt}data/workspace/forge/<name>@{utt} (the coder's file
tools reach @{tt}data/workspace/@{utt}); the webhook's bindings, inbox,
delivery keys and hourly counts in @{tt}data/forge/events.db@{utt}, shared
by the API and every @{tt}agent-cli@{utt} process. Design and measured
platform facts: @{" docs/concept.md " link docs/concept.md},
@{" docs/facts.md " link docs/facts.md}. The live tests run against a
GitLab test instance (@{tt}tests/live/gitlab/@{utt}) or a GitHub
repository, only with their environment set; the rest runs against fakes.
@endnode

@node gaps "Known gaps"
@{h1}Known gaps

@{bullet}@{b}One bot for every listed user.@{ub} Whoever is in
@{tt}allowed_users@{utt} pushes, comments and merges as the same bot; the
platform cannot tell them apart. The tools stay in the tool list of users
who are not listed -- they are refused when they call one.
@{bullet}@{b}The token is no secret from the coder's shell.@{ub} It lives
in @{tt}config/secrets.env@{utt}, which the coder's shell can read like any
file. What forge guarantees is narrower: its own git calls carry the token
only as a credential for the platform's origin, and push one branch under
the prefix, unforced. The binding limit is the token's role on the
platform.
@{bullet}@{b}A merge request is the prompt's to judge.@{ub} The tools
cannot see who assigned an issue or whether you asked for a merge; a prompt
injection in an issue could still reach @{tt}pr_merge@{utt}. What the tool
enforces is the list in @{" pr_merge " link merge}. A hold written in a
plain comment is the skill's to respect, not the tool's.
@{bullet}@{b}A plain-HTTP api_url sends the token unencrypted@{ub}, to the
API and to git. It is allowed, with one warning per host in the log at
start (none for localhost); only an http clone URL behind an https API is
refused.
@{bullet}@{b}GitHub CI can read green early@{ub} where a CI other than
Actions registers its checks late; Actions runs count before their jobs
exist.
@{bullet}@{b}GitLab merges with the project's merge method@{ub}; forge can
only ask for a squash, and refuses @{tt}rebase@{utt} there.
@{bullet}@{b}CI waits by blocking@{ub}: @{tt}ci_status@{utt} holds the call
up to @{tt}wait_s@{utt}; a longer pipeline needs another call.
@{bullet}@{b}Downstream pipelines@{ub} are one job with a note; a request
from a fork shows the fork's jobs without logs, or only the pipeline's
state where the token cannot read the fork.
@{bullet}@{b}A cancelled call keeps its clone locked until git ends@{ub},
within one process; the API and an @{tt}agent-cli@{utt} on the same clone
rely on git's own lock files.
@{bullet}@{b}GitHub:@{ub} @{tt}related_prs@{utt} of an issue is null; a
check of another CI system has no log; a fork's branch cannot be checked
out (only the project's own branches are fetched).
@{bullet}@{b}Webhook:@{ub} GitLab only. A comment on something nobody works
on starts nothing unless it mentions the bot; a failed pipeline of a branch
nobody works on wakes nobody.
@endnode
